1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:05,100
Water enters a plant through
its root hair cells by osmosis,

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:07,640
moving across selectively permeable membranes

3
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:10,280
from higher to lower water potential.

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:13,340
It then travels through the root into the xylem,

5
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:17,740
where a continuous column of liquid water
extends upward toward the leaves.

6
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:20,320
Inside the leaf, water evaporates

7
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:24,420
from the moist walls of
mesophyll cells into tiny air spaces.

8
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:29,420
The resulting water vapour diffuses
through open stomata into the atmosphere.

9
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:33,390
Evaporation creates tension, or negative pressure,

10
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:37,790
which pulls the remaining water upward
through the xylem, against gravity.

11
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:42,830
Cohesion, caused largely by
hydrogen bonds between water molecules,

12
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:45,410
helps maintain the continuous water column.

13
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,860
Adhesion attracts water molecules
to the xylem walls.

14
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,360
Guard cells regulate the stomata,

15
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:56,320
balancing carbon dioxide uptake for
photosynthesis against water loss.

16
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:00,160
Together, these processes
produce the transpiration stream,

17
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,380
carrying water from roots to leaves.
